{"key": "5b37a025caefd97d", "prompt": "Lower back pain when doing squats", "chosen": "Lower back pain during squats is a common issue that can be caused by a variety of factors, including:\n\n1. **Poor form**: If you're not using proper technique, you may put unnecessary strain on your lower back.\n2. **Weak glutes and core**: Your gluteus maximus and abdominal muscles play a crucial role in stabilizing the pelvis and supporting the spine during squats. Weakness in these areas can lead to poor form and put additional stress on the lower back.\n3. **Overreaching or overstretching**: If you're reaching too far forward with your torso, you may be putting unnecessary strain on your lower back.\n\nTo alleviate lower back pain when doing squats:\n\n1. **Check your form**: Make sure you're using proper technique:\n\t* Keep your back straight and core engaged.\n\t* Squeeze your glutes as you descend into the squat.\n\t* Avoid letting your knees extend past your toes.\n2. **Strengthen your glutes and core**:\n\t* Incorporate exercises like squats, lunges, deadlifts, and planks to target these areas.\n\t* Engage in exercises that strengthen your abdominal muscles, such as sit-ups or Russian twists.\n3. **Incorporate lower back strengthening exercises**:\n\t* Supermans: Lie on your stomach with arms extended in front of you. Lift your arms, shoulders, and legs off the ground, holding for 5-10 seconds.\n\t* Bird dogs: Start on your hands and knees. Lift one arm and the opposite leg off the ground, holding for a few seconds before switching sides.\n4. **Adjust the depth and weight**:\n\t* If you're feeling pain during squats, try reducing the depth or weight to alleviate some of the stress on your lower back.\n5. **Stretch and foam roll**:\n\t* Regularly stretch your hamstrings, hip flexors, and quadriceps to improve flexibility.\n\t* Use a foam roller to release tension in these areas.\n\nAdditional tips:\n\n1. **Warm up thoroughly**: Before starting squats, warm up with 10-15 