{"key": "40b47bfc1fdacbd4", "prompt": "Just did first week of squats and now my knee feels like it's been put through a wringer. Is that just normal or should I be worried? Doc said something about patella tracking... think that's what it was called. Any advice on how to deal with this, please?! Don't want to mess up and get injured.", "chosen": "The dreaded squat knee pain! It's not uncommon, especially if you're new to squats or haven't done them in a while. The good news is that it's usually a sign of your body adapting to the demands of the exercise, rather than an imminent injury.\n\nPatellar tracking (patellofemoral pain syndrome) refers to the wear and tear on the kneecap (patella) as it moves up and down the femur (thigh bone). This can cause discomfort, especially in the front or sides of the knee. It's often related to poor squat form, weak hip muscles, or overuse.\n\nTo alleviate the pain, try these suggestions:\n\n1. **Reassess your squat form**: Make sure you're not putting unnecessary stress on your knees by:\n\t* Keeping your weight too far forward.\n\t* Allowing your knees to extend past your toes (past 90 degrees).\n\t* Not keeping your back straight or core engaged.\n\t* Letting your hips sag or your lower back arch.\n2. **Strengthen your hip muscles**: Weak glutes and hip abductors can contribute to patellar tracking issues. Incorporate exercises like:\n\t* Glute bridges\n\t* Hip thrusts\n\t* Step-ups (using a box or bench)\n\t* Side lunges\n3. **Incorporate knee-friendly exercises**: Add some low-impact activities that target the quadriceps and hamstrings, such as:\n\t* Straight leg raises\n\t* Leg press (machine-based)\n\t* Seated leg curls and extensions\n4. **Take it easy for a bit**: Reduce the intensity and frequency of your squats for 1-2 weeks to allow your body to recover.\n5. **Use knee support or orthotics**: Consider using a donut-shaped cushion or a patellar tracking sleeve to provide extra support and reduce friction between the knee