{"key": "7c047c0178749d5c", "prompt": "Knee is throbbing after squats today... can't tell if it's normal or if I've done some damage. Think I read that patellofemoral pain syndrome is caused by poor form, overtraining, or just being a lazy bum (okay maybe not the last one). Is that what this is?!", "chosen": "Knee throbbing after squats can be concerning, so let's assess the situation carefully.\n\nPatellofemoral pain syndrome (PFPS) is indeed a common issue related to knee pain. The causes of PFPS are multifaceted and can include:\n\n1. **Poor form**: If your squat form is off, especially if you're letting your knees extend past your toes or not keeping your weight in the middle of your feet, it can put additional stress on your knee joint.\n2. **Overtraining**: Doing too many reps or sets of squats without adequate recovery time can lead to fatigue and increased risk of injury.\n3. **Biomechanical issues**: Weak quadriceps muscles, tight IT band, or flat feet can contribute to PFPS.\n4. **Repetitive stress**: Performing the same movement repeatedly, like deep squats, can put repetitive stress on your knee joint.\n\nHowever, there are other potential causes of knee pain after squats, such as:\n\n1. **Overload**: If you've recently increased the weight or intensity of your squats, it may be causing micro-tears in your muscles and connective tissues.\n2. **Improper warm-up or cool-down**: Not properly warming up or cooling down can increase the risk of injury.\n3. **Underlying conditions**: Pre-existing conditions like tendinitis, ligament sprains, or meniscal tears could be contributing to your knee pain.\n\nTo determine if you've done some damage, pay attention to the following:\n\n* The intensity and duration of the pain\n* Any swelling, redness, or bruising around the affected area\n* Whether the pain is constant or intermittent\n\nIn the meantime, consider these steps to help alleviate the discomfort:\n\n1. **Rest**: Give your knee a break and take a day or two 