{"key": "4295454f5e97621d", "prompt": "Ugh, my knee hurts after squats today... feel like I've been run over by a truck. Think it might be patellar tracking issue... read that it's caused by poor form, but can't seem to figure out what exactly went wrong. Do you guys have any advice on how to deal with this? Should I just take some time off or stretch more?", "chosen": "Knee pain after squats is never fun! Patellar tracking issues can be frustrating, but let's figure out what went wrong and how to fix it.\n\n**Re-evaluate your squat form:**\n\n1. **Foot position:** Ensure your feet are shoulder-width apart or slightly wider, with your weight evenly distributed between both feet.\n2. **Knee alignment:** Make sure your knees are tracking over your toes, not inward (toward each other). This is a common mistake that can put pressure on the patella.\n3. **Hip position:** Keep your hips back and down, avoiding letting them sag or lift too high. This will help maintain proper knee alignment.\n4. **Weight distribution:** Focus on keeping weight in your heels rather than your toes.\n5. **Depth of squat:** Aim for a depth that's deep enough to engage your glutes and hamstrings but not so low that you put excessive strain on your knees.\n\n**Additional tips:**\n\n1. **Strengthen your core and glutes:** Weakness in these areas can contribute to poor form and increased stress on the knee joint.\n2. **Stretch and foam roll regularly:** Focus on exercises like lunges, leg press, and calf raises to strengthen the muscles around your knee. Additionally, incorporate foam rolling or self-myofascial release into your routine to help relax tight muscles.\n3. **Work on patellar mobility:** Exercises like step-ups, single-leg squats, and wall sits can help improve patellar tracking and reduce pain.\n4. **Consider modifying your squat:** If you're finding it challenging to maintain proper form, try modifying the exercise by using a shorter range of motion or reducing the weight.\n\nIf pain